    "prespecified": "Fixed 2026-11-25, before any result (Rule 22, #758). The ladder of the #785 pilot: the same 14 arms on the same specimen (track-p head 52fff0de, the #1209 stair-scum extension), tier verysmall (classes-1800 starts 1-45), disjoint from the pilot's classes-18 slice and from the other tier's slice. The pilot's reads stand as the gate: scum0 matched ship's CL5 with 0 deaths and -10,800 median turns to L5; ratio1 killed 2; ratio4 cost 6 CL5 (p 0.031); eight dials never bound at 18 starts - this tier measures whether that was tier size. SAFETY: the owner expects scum0 (scumming fully off) to be where rare deaths would show; its read reports every death with cause, and scum0 cannot be adopted while its deaths exceed ship's at this tier. Every other arm is held to deaths-0 strictly (an arm whose deaths rise cannot be adopted however fast it levels). Scumming stays ON as shipped - \"scum saves lives but kills the attention\" (owner) - only its parameters may be tuned. Gate: no config_error; one binary; each arm's values in params-core.json; every cell ends in death, turn_cap or borg_stopped; identical plays per arm. Progress endpoint CL5 reached by 100,000 turns - known attainment out of all starts, deaths, unfinished and alive at the cutoff with CL5 reported separately; DL3/DL5 as diagnostics; median first-turn-at-level-5 where recorded. Report per arm against ship: CL5, paired gained/lost (exact McNemar), deaths and causes, rescued/newly killed, identical plays (a dial that replays ship here is \"not tested\", not \"no effect\" - RULES 18), median first-L5. Tier verysmall (RULES 14): the gate between the pilot and the medium payload. Descriptive (RULES 14).",
